WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.297
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:06.045 --> 00:00:08.670
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: When you have
a data-intensive applications,

00:00:08.670 --> 00:00:11.580
such as health insurance, and
your on-premise traditional

00:00:11.580 --> 00:00:15.090
data warehouse does not quite
fit the pace and the wallet

00:00:15.090 --> 00:00:17.325
with which your data science
team wants to innovate,

00:00:17.325 --> 00:00:21.120
you know it's time to migrate
your data warehouse to Cloud.

00:00:21.120 --> 00:00:24.000
So today, I invited
Gary Morreale, director

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:26.040
of Data Services
team, to share with us

00:00:26.040 --> 00:00:28.980
how they migrated their
data warehouse to BigQuery

00:00:28.980 --> 00:00:30.450
to innovate faster.

00:00:30.450 --> 00:00:31.230
Hi, Gary.

00:00:31.230 --> 00:00:32.130
Welcome to the show.

00:00:32.130 --> 00:00:32.880
GARY MORREALE: Hi.

00:00:32.880 --> 00:00:34.000
Thanks for having me.

00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:36.790
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: Well, let's
start with what is your role.

00:00:36.790 --> 00:00:37.623
GARY MORREALE: Sure.

00:00:37.623 --> 00:00:40.350
What my team does, the
Data Services team,

00:00:40.350 --> 00:00:43.570
is we support all of the
databases at the organization.

00:00:43.570 --> 00:00:46.140
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: So I imagine
it is large amount of data.

00:00:46.140 --> 00:00:49.015
As a data team, what's your
team's biggest challenge?

00:00:49.015 --> 00:00:50.640
GARY MORREALE: So
our biggest challenge

00:00:50.640 --> 00:00:52.890
is getting that data
into our databases

00:00:52.890 --> 00:00:55.660
and then getting it back
out in a timely fashion

00:00:55.660 --> 00:00:57.370
so we can meet the
businesses' needs.

00:00:57.370 --> 00:00:59.245
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: So
how much amount of data

00:00:59.245 --> 00:01:00.370
are we talking about here?

00:01:00.370 --> 00:01:02.578
GARY MORREALE: We're talking
about terabytes of data.

00:01:02.578 --> 00:01:04.440
And it's continuously growing.

00:01:04.440 --> 00:01:06.840
As everybody in the
industry probably knows,

00:01:06.840 --> 00:01:09.490
it's got an exponential
curve to it.

00:01:09.490 --> 00:01:11.425
And it's getting
bigger all the time.

00:01:11.425 --> 00:01:13.800
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: So it sounds
like the biggest goal was

00:01:13.800 --> 00:01:16.500
to really create a
forward-looking system

00:01:16.500 --> 00:01:21.690
to store this ever-growing data
in a secure, scalable, and also

00:01:21.690 --> 00:01:23.200
cost-effective manner.

00:01:23.200 --> 00:01:25.170
GARY MORREALE: So getting
that data in and out

00:01:25.170 --> 00:01:28.330
and making that data available
to all of our users--

00:01:28.330 --> 00:01:31.380
we have thousands of users
and terabytes of data.

00:01:31.380 --> 00:01:34.620
And meeting those challenges
was getting harder and harder

00:01:34.620 --> 00:01:36.810
in our existing environment.

00:01:36.810 --> 00:01:39.360
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: So
migration from on-premise data

00:01:39.360 --> 00:01:42.930
warehouse to BigQuery
is quite an undertaking,

00:01:42.930 --> 00:01:46.050
definitely not flip of a
switch kind of migration.

00:01:46.050 --> 00:01:49.290
You need to make sure
your downstream systems--

00:01:49.290 --> 00:01:51.660
in this case, thousands
of downstream systems--

00:01:51.660 --> 00:01:56.100
don't break due to inconsistent
results in your final tables

00:01:56.100 --> 00:01:57.490
after your migration.

00:01:57.490 --> 00:02:01.120
So how did you approach
this migration?

00:02:01.120 --> 00:02:03.510
GARY MORREALE: So we
engaged Datametica,

00:02:03.510 --> 00:02:06.240
one of Google's
business partners,

00:02:06.240 --> 00:02:09.180
to help us complete an
assessment of everything

00:02:09.180 --> 00:02:12.840
we have today, which included
not just our data warehouse,

00:02:12.840 --> 00:02:15.840
but all the applications that
use it and all of the processes

00:02:15.840 --> 00:02:18.300
that pull data in
and take data out.

00:02:18.300 --> 00:02:21.510
And they were able to
come back and give us

00:02:21.510 --> 00:02:24.360
a complete picture
of what it was we

00:02:24.360 --> 00:02:25.862
were going to be dealing with.

00:02:25.862 --> 00:02:28.320
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: Well, after
hearing that solid migration

00:02:28.320 --> 00:02:31.003
plan, I'm very excited to
dive into the migration

00:02:31.003 --> 00:02:31.545
architecture.

00:02:31.545 --> 00:02:34.710
It sounds like this is an
ongoing migration right now.

00:02:34.710 --> 00:02:35.463
Walk me through.

00:02:35.463 --> 00:02:37.380
GARY MORREALE: So the
first thing we had to do

00:02:37.380 --> 00:02:39.360
was put the foundation in place.

00:02:39.360 --> 00:02:42.450
So that involved getting our
networking set up, getting

00:02:42.450 --> 00:02:44.550
the environment
in Google set up,

00:02:44.550 --> 00:02:47.580
all the connections between
the different environments here

00:02:47.580 --> 00:02:50.520
and there between our
data center and Google--

00:02:50.520 --> 00:02:52.480
so we got all of that in place.

00:02:52.480 --> 00:02:54.300
And then once that
was there, then we

00:02:54.300 --> 00:02:56.730
were able to start working
on moving the data over.

00:02:56.730 --> 00:02:59.760
So we have all the data
in our existing system.

00:02:59.760 --> 00:03:05.850
And we used a transfer agent
to transfer to BigQuery moves.

00:03:05.850 --> 00:03:08.910
And that allowed us to pull
the data into Cloud Storage.

00:03:08.910 --> 00:03:13.200
And then from there, the data
went into data quality checks

00:03:13.200 --> 00:03:15.780
via Dataflow, which
allowed us to move

00:03:15.780 --> 00:03:17.550
the data into the environment.

00:03:17.550 --> 00:03:19.110
We were able to
set that scheduling

00:03:19.110 --> 00:03:23.700
up using Cloud Composer to do
scheduling and orchestration as

00:03:23.700 --> 00:03:24.690
well.

00:03:24.690 --> 00:03:26.670
Then we got it into
the staging environment

00:03:26.670 --> 00:03:29.580
in BigQuery, which allowed
us to push it into our core

00:03:29.580 --> 00:03:31.320
environment and
then our information

00:03:31.320 --> 00:03:34.080
layer so that our end
users would have access

00:03:34.080 --> 00:03:35.370
to everything.

00:03:35.370 --> 00:03:37.680
And then we have to worry
about keeping everything

00:03:37.680 --> 00:03:40.800
in sync because while we're in
the migration process, which

00:03:40.800 --> 00:03:43.380
will go on for some time,
we need to make sure

00:03:43.380 --> 00:03:45.487
that what was happening
in the old system

00:03:45.487 --> 00:03:47.070
is what's happening
in the new system.

00:03:47.070 --> 00:03:52.410
And Pelican is the
tool with a GKE cluster

00:03:52.410 --> 00:03:56.637
that allows us to do that
comparison between the two

00:03:56.637 --> 00:03:58.470
and make sure things
stay in sync, which has

00:03:58.470 --> 00:04:00.510
been a really good feature--

00:04:00.510 --> 00:04:03.190
gives us a lot of confidence
in what we're doing.

00:04:03.190 --> 00:04:04.860
And then the end
result of all of this

00:04:04.860 --> 00:04:09.510
is that our consumers on the
final end of this journey--

00:04:09.510 --> 00:04:12.960
people using SQL Server
Reporting Services, Tableau,

00:04:12.960 --> 00:04:14.880
other applications
we've written,

00:04:14.880 --> 00:04:16.980
or third-party applications--

00:04:16.980 --> 00:04:20.730
are able to now just point at
BigQuery instead of pointing

00:04:20.730 --> 00:04:23.610
at our existing system and
do the same things that they

00:04:23.610 --> 00:04:25.355
were doing previously.

00:04:25.355 --> 00:04:26.730
PRIYANKA VERGADIA:
Well, awesome.

00:04:26.730 --> 00:04:27.780
That was a lot.

00:04:27.780 --> 00:04:30.930
Now, given your consumers
of the data, which

00:04:30.930 --> 00:04:34.530
is the data scientists and
the business analytics team,

00:04:34.530 --> 00:04:37.800
you have this modern data
warehouse in BigQuery

00:04:37.800 --> 00:04:38.910
at this point.

00:04:38.910 --> 00:04:41.370
How do you ingest
and process the data?

00:04:41.370 --> 00:04:45.810
And then how do data science
and business analytics team

00:04:45.810 --> 00:04:49.470
do their job, which is analyze
this data and make predictions?

00:04:49.470 --> 00:04:50.370
GARY MORREALE: Sure.

00:04:50.370 --> 00:04:52.500
Well, we have a lot of
different data sources.

00:04:52.500 --> 00:04:56.190
Being a health insurer, we get
data from various providers,

00:04:56.190 --> 00:04:59.010
like hospitals and
doctors' offices,

00:04:59.010 --> 00:05:01.450
testing centers,
things like that.

00:05:01.450 --> 00:05:02.970
We also get data
from our members.

00:05:02.970 --> 00:05:05.220
And then we have a lot of
processing business partners

00:05:05.220 --> 00:05:07.780
that we work as well
as government entities.

00:05:07.780 --> 00:05:12.180
So we needed a way to set up
secure file transfers, which

00:05:12.180 --> 00:05:13.090
we can do.

00:05:13.090 --> 00:05:15.690
And we can load that
into Cloud Storage

00:05:15.690 --> 00:05:20.050
and then use API-based services,
as well, to do the same thing.

00:05:20.050 --> 00:05:23.010
And then we can move
that using Dataproc

00:05:23.010 --> 00:05:24.900
with our Hadoop-type
environments

00:05:24.900 --> 00:05:29.100
that we're used to using to
load the data up into BigQuery.

00:05:29.100 --> 00:05:31.230
So now we've got
everything in place.

00:05:31.230 --> 00:05:34.110
And the data scientists
can then use the tools

00:05:34.110 --> 00:05:36.600
that they're used to
using, which, in this case,

00:05:36.600 --> 00:05:39.660
would be the Vertex artificial
intelligence pipelines that

00:05:39.660 --> 00:05:41.370
are provided in the
Google environment.

00:05:41.370 --> 00:05:45.970
They use Jupyter Notebooks
to work with all of that.

00:05:45.970 --> 00:05:48.480
They're able to take
advantage of the fact

00:05:48.480 --> 00:05:50.550
that it's not a static
environment, like we

00:05:50.550 --> 00:05:51.310
had before.

00:05:51.310 --> 00:05:52.680
So they can add resources.

00:05:52.680 --> 00:05:57.930
For example, GPUs become a huge
benefit in this environment

00:05:57.930 --> 00:05:58.890
to data scientists.

00:05:58.890 --> 00:06:01.900
So they can dynamically
add them as needed.

00:06:01.900 --> 00:06:04.170
And then what they end
up doing in the end

00:06:04.170 --> 00:06:08.250
is they build models that
help us assess and do

00:06:08.250 --> 00:06:10.560
preventative care,
understand what's

00:06:10.560 --> 00:06:14.310
going on with our pharmacies,
what risks people may have,

00:06:14.310 --> 00:06:17.170
what kind of fraud
might be going on.

00:06:17.170 --> 00:06:19.752
It just allows them
to do the magic

00:06:19.752 --> 00:06:21.210
that it is-- the
data scientists do

00:06:21.210 --> 00:06:23.220
and understanding
how all this data

00:06:23.220 --> 00:06:25.842
can be put to use to make
our members' health better.

00:06:25.842 --> 00:06:27.300
PRIYANKA VERGADIA:
That is amazing.

00:06:27.300 --> 00:06:29.670
So what is next in
this architecture?

00:06:29.670 --> 00:06:31.285
And what's your
team working on now?

00:06:31.285 --> 00:06:32.910
GARY MORREALE: So
the nice thing is now

00:06:32.910 --> 00:06:35.070
that we have this
foundation in place

00:06:35.070 --> 00:06:37.810
with the security, the
networking, all of that,

00:06:37.810 --> 00:06:41.880
we can start to look at what
other types of opportunities

00:06:41.880 --> 00:06:42.540
there might be.

00:06:42.540 --> 00:06:45.870
So we've been looking at
voice recognition software.

00:06:45.870 --> 00:06:49.740
We've been looking at different
types of reporting tools

00:06:49.740 --> 00:06:51.600
that are provided up there.

00:06:51.600 --> 00:06:55.590
There's just a whole host
of different applications

00:06:55.590 --> 00:06:57.420
that are provided on
the Google platform

00:06:57.420 --> 00:07:02.700
that we can now easily put
into place compared to before,

00:07:02.700 --> 00:07:06.280
having to buy different
hardware in our own data center.

00:07:06.280 --> 00:07:08.532
So it just presents a
lot of opportunities.

00:07:08.532 --> 00:07:09.990
And we're looking
at a lot of them.

00:07:09.990 --> 00:07:12.490
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: Well, now
that everything is in one place

00:07:12.490 --> 00:07:15.330
and your infrastructure
layers are all

00:07:15.330 --> 00:07:20.410
set up, you can just innovate on
top of that, which is amazing.

00:07:20.410 --> 00:07:21.780
Thank you so much, Gary.

00:07:21.780 --> 00:07:24.930
And we congratulate you for
what you have accomplished, you

00:07:24.930 --> 00:07:26.400
and the team have accomplished.

00:07:26.400 --> 00:07:28.620
And we look forward
to seeing more updates

00:07:28.620 --> 00:07:29.665
on this architecture.

00:07:29.665 --> 00:07:30.540
GARY MORREALE: Great.

00:07:30.540 --> 00:07:31.040
Thank you.

00:07:31.040 --> 00:07:32.650
It's been great
talking to you today.

00:07:32.650 --> 00:07:35.233
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: If you have
